MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m18",
  "summary": "Explain how suspension, steering and wheel alignment work, inspect and service the parts safely, and diagnose wander, pull, vibration, noise and uneven tyre wear.",
  "outcomes": ["Name the main suspension types and explain the job of springs, dampers and anti-roll bars.","Inspect bushes, ball joints, dampers and springs, and work safely with coil springs.","Describe rack-and-pinion and steering-box systems and hydraulic and electric power assistance.","Explain toe, camber, caster and thrust angle and how each one affects tyre wear and handling.","Balance a wheel, check runout and fit a tyre safely.","Diagnose pull, wander, shimmy, noises and tyre wear patterns in a logical order."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Springs carry the weight. Dampers (shock absorbers) control the bounce. Neither works well alone.","Check ball joints, tie-rod ends and bushes for play with the wheel off the ground, as the manual says.","Never remove a coil spring without a proper spring compressor, correctly fitted.","Tighten bushes and suspension arm bolts with the vehicle on its wheels at ride height.","Toe is the in or out angle of the wheels seen from above. Wrong toe wears tyres quickly.","Camber is the lean of the wheel seen from the front. Caster is the forward or backward tilt of the steering axis.","Uneven tyre wear is a clue. Read it before you change a part.","After replacing steering or suspension parts, the wheel alignment must be checked.","Disconnect the battery and wait before working near the steering column on a car with airbags."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m18-l1", "title": "Suspension types", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The suspension connects the wheels to the body. It keeps the tyres on the road, carries the weight of the vehicle and gives the passengers a comfortable ride. A good mechanic knows the main parts and types so that the inspection and the repair make sense."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"macpherson-strut"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Springs:** carry the weight and absorb bumps. They can be coil springs, leaf springs, torsion bars or air springs.","**Dampers (shock absorbers):** control the movement of the spring. Without a damper, the body keeps bouncing after a bump. A damper turns movement into heat through oil forced through small holes.","**Anti-roll bar (stabiliser bar):** a bar linking the left and right sides that reduces body roll in corners. It is joined to the suspension by drop links.","**Arms, links and bushes:** locate the wheel. Rubber or polyurethane **bushes** absorb noise and allow small movements.","**Ball joints:** swivelling joints that let the wheel steer and move up and down together."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common types"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Where it is used"],"rows":[["MacPherson strut","A strut combines the damper and spring. A lower arm locates the bottom of the wheel","Front of most small and medium cars"],["Double wishbone","Two arms, upper and lower, locate the wheel","Performance cars and some SUVs"],["Multi-link","Several links control each wheel in different directions","Rear of many modern cars"],["Leaf spring and solid axle","A rigid axle held by leaf springs","Pick-ups, trucks and vans"],["Torsion beam","A cross beam twists to act as a spring and anti-roll bar","Rear of small front-wheel-drive cars"]],"caption":"Suspension types"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ride and handling"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Soft springs give comfort but the body leans more. Stiff springs give firmer handling but a harder ride. The designer finds a balance. Heavy loads, which are common on our roads, compress the springs and change the alignment and the handling, so overloaded vehicles wear parts fast."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Independent and solid-axle suspension"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Independent (each wheel moves alone)","Better ride and grip. A bump on one wheel does not affect the other. Less unsprung weight.","More parts and cost. More joints and bushes to wear."],["Solid (beam) axle","Strong and simple, good for heavy loads and rough roads. Cheap to repair.","A bump on one wheel moves the other. More unsprung weight, rougher ride."]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **unsprung mass** is the weight that is not carried by the springs: wheels, tyres, brakes, hubs and part of the arms and axle. A lighter unsprung mass lets the wheel follow the road better. Heavy wheels, brake parts and a solid axle make the wheel bounce more. The **sprung mass** is the body and everything it carries."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Anti-roll bars and bump stops"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **anti-roll bar** is a torsion spring that twists when one side of the axle moves more than the other. It resists body roll in corners without making the ride stiffer over bumps that move both wheels together. A thicker bar, or one with stiffer bushes, reduces roll more. The bar is held to the body by rubber bushes and linked to the arms by **drop links**. A worn bush or a broken link gives a clunk over uneven roads and more roll in corners. A **bump stop** is a rubber or foam block that limits the movement of the suspension and cushions the end of its travel. A crumbled bump stop shows the suspension has been bottoming, often from overloading or a weak spring."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Many bars have a **split bush** held by a clamp. Fit the bush with the slit to the correct side and lubricate it with rubber lubricant.","A bar that is bent from a kerb strike makes the vehicle lean one way and the steering feel odd.","Replace drop links in pairs where possible."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The spring carries the load and the damper controls the movement. A worn damper makes a car with good springs bounce, wander and wear its tyres unevenly."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Look at the stance of the vehicle on level ground. If one corner sits low, suspect a tired or broken spring before looking at anything else."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l2", "title": "Suspension geometry, springs and handling", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A suspension is a balance of comfort, grip and control. Understanding a few simple ideas, the stiffness of the spring, the frequency of the ride, the transfer of weight in a corner and the way the wheel moves, helps you to explain why a vehicle handles as it does, and to judge what a worn or wrongly fitted part will do. This lesson gives these ideas with numbers."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Spring rate and ride frequency"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **spring rate** is the force needed to compress a spring by a certain distance, in newtons per millimetre. A stiffer spring has a higher rate. The body on its springs bounces at a **natural frequency**, which depends on the spring rate and the mass it carries. Passengers find a frequency of about 1.0 to 1.5 cycles per second comfortable. It is close to a walking pace. Much lower feels sea-sick, and much higher is harsh."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Natural frequency (Hz) = 1 ÷ (2π) × √(spring rate ÷ mass)","caption":"Use newtons per metre for the rate and kilograms for the mass"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Ride frequency of one corner","steps":["One corner carries a sprung mass of 350 kg. The wheel rate of the spring is 25 N/mm, which is 25,000 N/m.","Frequency = 1 ÷ 6.283 × √(25,000 ÷ 350) = 0.159 × √71.4 = 0.159 × 8.45 = 1.34 Hz.","If the vehicle is loaded with 300 kg more, spread over four wheels, the mass at one corner rises to 425 kg. The frequency falls to 0.159 × √(25,000 ÷ 425) = 0.159 × 7.67 = 1.22 Hz.","The ride gets softer as the load goes up, and the body sits lower, which is why the dampers must be strong enough for the load. A tired spring with a lower rate gives a lower frequency and a bouncy, floating ride."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Weight transfer in corners"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When a car turns, the body tends to lean outwards, and the weight on the outside wheels increases while the inside wheels become lighter. This **weight transfer** depends on the height of the centre of gravity and the track width. A higher car with a narrower track transfers more weight, leans more, and may tip."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Weight transfer (N) = mass × lateral acceleration × centre of gravity height ÷ track width"},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much weight shifts in a hard corner?","steps":["A car of 1,400 kg corners at 0.6 g, which is 0.6 × 9.81 = 5.89 m/s². The centre of gravity is 0.55 m high, and the track is 1.5 m.","Force = 1,400 × 5.89 = 8,240 N. Weight transfer = 8,240 × 0.55 ÷ 1.5 = 3,021 N, equal to about 308 kg.","The outside tyres carry 308 kg more, and the inside tyres 308 kg less, between the two sides. A tyre can only provide so much grip, so the more the load is uneven, the less total grip the car has. Stiff anti-roll bars, low centre of gravity and wide track reduce body roll and weight transfer."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Understeer and oversteer"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Term","What happens","Common causes"],"rows":[["Understeer","The front tyres lose grip first. The car runs wide when the driver turns in.","Worn or under-inflated front tyres, too much front weight, a worn front damper, or too much speed"],["Oversteer","The rear tyres lose grip first. The tail slides out.","Worn rear tyres or low pressures, a broken rear damper or spring, a heavy rear load, a sudden lift-off or braking"],["Neutral","Front and rear grip together","Balanced tyres and suspension"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Safe manufacturers design a slight understeer, because it is easier for an ordinary driver to correct by slowing down. Faults that change the balance, such as a worn rear bush, a broken spring or different tyres on the two axles, make a car unpredictable. Always fit the better tyres on the rear axle, to avoid oversteer."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How a wheel moves"},
      {"t":"p","text":"As the suspension moves up and down, the wheel does not just rise. Its camber and toe change a little. A well-designed suspension keeps these changes small, so the tyre stays flat on the road. In corners, the body roll and wheel movement change the camber. A little negative camber on the outside wheel helps its grip. Worn or bent arms, bushes and bump stops change the geometry, which is why a repair to a suspension must be followed by an alignment check."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ride height and load"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Ride height** is the distance between the body and the ground, measured at set points. The manual gives a figure. A low ride height from tired springs changes the alignment angles, and the rubber bump stop hits sooner.","**Overloading** compresses the springs and shortens the life of parts, and makes the steering lighter, with less traction.","**Uneven height** from side to side means a weak or broken spring or a failed damper."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A spring's rate and the mass it carries decide the ride. A tired spring, an overloaded car and a worn damper all change it, and they all change the handling too."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Measure the ride height at each corner and compare it with the manual or with the other side. It shows weak springs quickly."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l3", "title": "Suspension inspection and repair", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Rough roads and potholes punish suspension parts, so inspection is one of the most common jobs in the workshop. This lesson shows a safe and thorough way to do it."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"suspension-checkpoints"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Inspection steps"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Park on level ground and look at the stance. Compare the height of the left and right sides.","Do a **bounce test**: push down hard on a corner and release. A good damper lets the body rise once and settle. A worn one keeps bouncing.","Raise the vehicle safely on a lift or on axle stands placed under the jacking points.","Look at the dampers for oil leaks, dents and damaged rubber mounts.","Check coil springs for cracks, broken ends or rust, and leaf springs for broken leaves and shifted centre bolts.","Check the bushes for cracks, splits and movement.","Check the ball joints and tie-rod ends for play, using the method in the workshop manual. Some joints are checked with the wheel loaded and some with it unloaded.","Check the anti-roll bar links and bushes, which often make a knock over bumps."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Noises and what they suggest"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Knock over bumps:** worn drop links, ball joints, strut top mounts or dampers.","**Creaks when turning or on a speed bump:** dry bushes, ball joints or strut mounts.","**Clunk when braking or moving off:** worn lower arm bushes."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Repair rules"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Replace dampers and springs in **pairs on the same axle**, so that the left and right sides act the same. Use new self-locking nuts and bolts when the manual says so. Fit the new parts, but only tighten bushes and arm bolts fully **with the vehicle on its wheels at ride height**, or with the suspension loaded. If you tighten them with the wheels hanging, the rubber bush is twisted in normal use and tears early."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking ball joints and bushes: what to look for"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Test","Fault"],"rows":[["Lower ball joint (load-carrying)","Raise the wheel, use a lever under the tyre and watch the joint","Vertical movement of the joint stud in its socket, or a split boot"],["Upper ball joint or steering joint (follower)","Rock the wheel at 12 and 6 o'clock, with the brake applied, and watch","Free play or a clunk in the joint"],["Tie-rod end","Hold the joint and move the wheel, or shake the rod","Free movement at the ball"],["Wishbone and arm bushes","Lever on the arm and watch the bush for movement","Cracked or squashed rubber, sideways movement"],["Strut top mount","Look at the mount with the bonnet open and turn the wheel with the vehicle on the ground","Clunk, bearing roughness, movement of the strut"]],"caption":"The manual says whether a joint is loaded or unloaded for a test. Always check."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Record the play found in millimetres where the manual gives a limit. A joint at the limit should be replaced, as it wears rapidly after that. Boots that are split let in grit and water, and a joint with a split boot is replaced unless it has just happened and the joint is clean and unworn."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A compressed coil spring stores huge energy. Use only a spring compressor that is in good condition, fitted correctly, and never remove the strut nut until the spring is fully held by the compressor. Never work under a vehicle supported by a jack alone."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Fully tighten rubber bushes only at ride height. This is one of the most common reasons for new bushes failing early."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l4", "title": "Dampers, springs and air suspension", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The spring holds the car up, and the damper stops it bouncing. Both wear, and a worn damper is one of the commonest hidden faults on any vehicle. Modern vehicles may also have air springs and electronic dampers. This lesson explains how each works, how to test them and how to deal with air suspension safely."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How a damper works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A damper is a piston in a cylinder of oil. As the suspension moves, the piston forces oil through small valves, and the resistance turns the movement into heat. The damper resists **rebound** (extension) more than **bump** (compression), so that the spring can compress easily over a bump but the body does not spring back too fast. The oil must not foam, so many dampers carry gas at low pressure."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","Design","Features"],"rows":[["Twin-tube","A working cylinder inside an outer reserve tube. A low-pressure gas cell or air in the outer tube.","Common and inexpensive. Comfortable. Can fade under hard use."],["Monotube (gas-pressure)","A single tube with a floating piston and high-pressure gas","Better cooling and response. Firmer. Sensitive to damage to the tube."],["Adaptive (variable)","Electronic valves or magnetic fluid change the damping","Switches between comfort and sport, and adjusts to the road"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Signs of a worn damper"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["The body continues to bounce after a bump, or the vehicle floats on a long undulation.","Oil leaking down the body of the damper (a damp film is normal, a wet streak with dust is a leak).","Uneven or scalloped tyre wear, called cupping.","The nose dives on braking or the tail squats on acceleration.","A knocking noise from worn mounts, bushes or a bent rod."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Why dampers wear out unnoticed","steps":["A damper does about 1,500 to 3,000 cycles every kilometre on a rough road. In 80,000 km, that is 120 to 240 million movements.","Its performance fades slowly, by about 10 to 20% in 50,000 km, so the driver adapts without noticing.","A damper at 50% of its original force still looks fine, but the stopping distance on a rough surface rises, as the tyres lose contact with the road. Test a damper at 50,000 to 80,000 km, and advise the customer in writing."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Coil springs and leaf springs"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Coil springs** may be straight, barrel-shaped or progressive (rate increases as it compresses). They sag with age, break near the ends and rust. A broken spring can scratch the tyre or the damper.","**Leaf springs** are a stack of curved steel strips. They can break a leaf, sag, move at the centre bolt or wear at the eyes and shackles. Check the shackle bushes and the U-bolts.","**Torsion bars** twist to act as springs. They are adjusted to set ride height, and a broken bar causes a corner to drop.","Replace springs in **pairs**, and use the correct rate for the vehicle's load."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Air suspension"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **air spring** is a rubber bellows filled with air at several bar. Height sensors tell the computer the ride height, and a **compressor** and **valve block** add or release air to keep it level, whatever the load. Many have a selectable height for off-road or for loading. The same system can lower the car at speed to cut drag."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Job"],"rows":[["Air springs (bags)","Carry the load and set the ride height"],["Compressor and dryer","Supply compressed air, with a dryer to remove moisture"],["Valve block","Directs air to or from each spring, and to the reservoir"],["Height sensors","Report each corner's height to the computer"],["Control module","Calculates the demand and runs the system"],["Air lines and fittings","Carry air. Push-in fittings are easy to damage."]]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Vehicle sags overnight at one corner","A leak in the bag, a line, a fitting or a valve"],["Compressor runs a lot, or for a long time","Leaks, a worn compressor or a faulty relay"],["Warning on the dash and the car stuck at one height","A height sensor or valve fault, a code in the module"],["Harsh ride","Bag perished or stuck, a dried-up dampers, or faulty sensors"],["Wet or frozen air lines","A failed dryer"]]},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Find a leak with **soapy water** on the connections and bags, and with the scan tool's leak test. The bags crack at the folds and at the ends with age.","Before raising or supporting a vehicle with air suspension, switch the system to **jacking** or **transport mode** with the scan tool or the switch, as the manual says. Otherwise the car can lower itself and trap or crush a person.","Do not pressurise a bag when it is off the vehicle without the correct support, as it can burst.","Do not fit an ordinary coil spring in place of an air spring unless a kit is approved."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A damper is a wear item. Replace in pairs, test at the service and tell the customer what you find, with the readings."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Before you lift or support a vehicle with air suspension, put it in the correct mode and support it on stands. A vehicle that lowers while you are underneath is crushing."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l5", "title": "Steering systems", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The steering lets the driver point the front wheels. It must be precise, light and safe. Failure of a steering part is a serious danger, so every worn part must be replaced and never repaired by welding or heating."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Steering wheel and column:** the column carries the movement from the driver to the steering gear. Modern columns collapse in a crash and contain the airbag, the clock spring and switches.","**Steering gear:** converts the turning of the wheel into side movement. The **rack and pinion** is the most common. A small gear (pinion) moves a toothed bar (rack). A **steering box** uses a worm and a moving nut, often in trucks and older vehicles.","**Tie rods and tie-rod ends:** connect the rack to the steering arms on the wheels. The outer joints are adjustable and set the toe.","**Steering arms and knuckles:** the parts that turn with the wheel."]},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"steering-rack"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Power assistance"},
      {"t":"table","head":["System","How it assists","Notes"],"rows":[["Hydraulic (HPS)","An engine-driven pump sends fluid to a piston in the rack","Needs power steering fluid and a belt. Watch for leaks and noise from low fluid"],["Electro-hydraulic","An electric motor drives the pump","No belt. Pump runs on demand"],["Electric (EPS)","An electric motor on the column or rack, controlled by a computer and a torque sensor","No fluid. Needs a sensor calibration after some repairs"]],"caption":"Power steering types"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Steering lock and airbags"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The steering lock is a pin in the column that stops the wheel turning when the key is out. Do not force a stuck lock, because you can break the column. On a vehicle with an airbag, the steering wheel contains a live airbag. **Disconnect the battery and wait for the time in the manual** before removing it. Never probe the airbag wiring and carry the airbag with the cover pointing away from you."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Steering ratio"},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much does the wheel turn for each degree of road wheel?","steps":["A steering wheel turns three full turns from lock to lock, which is 3 × 360 = 1,080°.","The front wheels turn about 35° each way, which is 70° of road wheel travel from lock to lock.","Steering ratio = 1,080 ÷ 70 = 15.4 to 1. The driver must turn the steering wheel 15.4° to turn the road wheels by 1°.","A lower ratio, around 12:1, gives a sharper and more responsive feel, but needs more effort. A higher ratio gives easier effort but more turning of the wheel."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The steering column"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **steering column** carries the movement from the wheel to the rack. A modern column is designed to **collapse** in a crash, by a sliding or crushing section, so it does not push the wheel into the driver. It often has a **tilt and telescopic** adjustment, locked by a lever or motor, and holds the ignition switch, the steering lock, the stalks and the clock spring. An **intermediate shaft** with universal joints, or a flexible joint (**rag joint**) of rubber, connects the column to the pinion. Wear in these joints causes play, a clunk when the wheel is turned back and forth, or a vibration."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check the column for movement at the top and for play in the intermediate joints.","A column that does not lock when the key is removed, or one that locks while driving, must be repaired at once.","The **clock spring** is a flat ribbon cable that lets the wheel turn and keeps the airbag and switches connected. Do not turn the steering with the wheel off, and centre the clock spring correctly when fitting."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Do not repair bent or cracked steering parts by welding or heating. Replace them. When the steering wheel is removed, do not turn the clock spring, because it can be broken, and mark its centre position."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A vehicle that has electric steering needs the battery to be healthy. A weak battery can make the steering heavy or give warnings."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l6", "title": "Steering geometry, power steering and electric steering in depth", "minutes": 6, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The steering is a safety-critical system. It must be accurate, give the driver feel and keep working if the assistance fails. This lesson explains how the wheels are turned to different angles in a corner, how hydraulic and electric power steering operate, and how to test and bleed them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Turning in a corner: Ackermann"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a turn the inner wheel follows a smaller circle than the outer wheel, so it must be steered through a larger angle. The steering arms are angled so that lines through the track rod ends meet near the rear axle, which gives this effect. This is the **Ackermann principle**. A bent steering arm or track rod changes it and wears the tyres in corners."},
      {"t":"example","title":"How different are the two wheel angles?","steps":["A car has a wheelbase of 2.6 m and a track of 1.5 m. It turns on a circle of radius 5.5 m measured at the centre of the rear axle.","The inner wheel angle = arctan (2.6 ÷ (5.5 − 0.75)) = arctan (0.547) = 28.7°.","The outer wheel angle = arctan (2.6 ÷ (5.5 + 0.75)) = arctan (0.416) = 22.6°.","The inner wheel turns 6.1° more than the outer. If both were parallel, the tyres would scrub and squeal, and wear very quickly."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hydraulic power steering"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine-driven **pump** makes oil pressure, typically 80 to 120 bar at full lock. A **rotary valve** at the pinion senses the force that the driver applies to the wheel and directs oil to one side of a piston on the rack, which pushes the rack in the direction wanted. The harder the driver turns, the more assistance. A **torsion bar** between the steering wheel and the pinion twists a little, and operates the valve. The system is simple and gives good feel, but it uses engine power all the time, even when driving straight."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Heavy steering, or whine on turning","Low fluid, an air leak into the pump, a slipping belt, a worn pump"],["Heavy steering to one side only","Worn seal in the rack or a damaged valve"],["Fluid leaks","Hoses, rack seals or boots, pump shaft seal"],["Groan or noise on full lock","Air in the system, a low level or the relief valve working"],["Steering wheel kicks or judders","Worn or stuck valve, bent rack, a slack belt"],["Foamy or dark fluid","Air getting in, or old and contaminated fluid"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking and bleeding hydraulic steering"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the fluid level with the engine off and cold, and look at the colour. Dark, foamy or burnt fluid needs replacing.","Look for leaks at the hoses, the rack and the pump. Look at the belt.","To bleed after a repair, fill the reservoir to the mark, and with the front wheels raised, turn the steering slowly lock to lock several times with the engine off. Do not hold on full lock.","Start the engine and turn lock to lock again slowly. Top up as the level falls. Repeat until there are no bubbles and the steering is quiet.","Check for leaks, and the level again."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Test","How","Result"],"rows":[["Pump pressure","Fit a gauge with a shut-off valve between the pump and the rack, and read the maximum pressure with the valve briefly closed. Do this only for a few seconds.","The pump should reach its relief pressure (often 80 to 120 bar). A low reading means a worn pump."],["Rack leak-down","With the engine running, turn to full lock. The pressure should stay.","A low pressure at full lock means an internal leak in the rack"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Electric power steering (EPS)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An electric motor assists the steering, either on the column, the pinion or the rack. A **torque sensor** in the column measures how hard the driver turns, and the control module decides how much help to give, based also on vehicle speed. The assistance is large when parking and small at speed. There is no pump, no fluid and no belt, and the system uses energy only when it helps. It can also add features such as lane-keeping steering, parking assistance and active return to centre."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Job"],"rows":[["Torque sensor","Measures twist in a torsion bar and tells the module the driver's effort"],["Steering angle sensor","Reports the position of the wheel"],["Motor and gearbox","Add torque. The current can be 30 to 80 A when parking."],["Control module","Calculates the assistance and monitors the system"],["Vehicle speed signal","Reduces assistance at speed"]]},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A low **battery voltage** reduces the assistance, or causes warnings, so check the battery and the charging first.","A fault in a sensor puts the system into a reduced-assist or **no-assist** mode, with a warning light. The steering is still possible but very heavy.","After replacing the rack, column, sensor or after an alignment, the **steering angle sensor** and the **torque sensor** may need to be **calibrated** or zeroed with a scan tool.","The motor and module carry high current. Disconnect the battery before working, and follow the manual.","Never turn the steering with the vehicle on stands and the engine running for a long time without a reason, since the motor can overheat."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checks on the rack and joints"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Look at the **gaiters** (rubber boots) at each end of the rack for splits and grease loss.","Check the inner tie-rod joints inside the gaiters by pushing and pulling on the tie rod.","Check the rack for **play**: move the steering wheel gently in both directions with the vehicle on its wheels and watch the wheels. A gap before the wheels move shows play in the column, the rack or the joints.","A **rack with play** should be adjusted, if it has an adjustment, or replaced."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Steering is a safety system. Any play, leak or sensor fault must be put right before the vehicle goes back on the road."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never weld, heat or straighten steering parts. Never leave a vehicle with a steering fault, a leak or a warning light without telling the customer and recording it in writing."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l7", "title": "Wheel alignment angles", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Wheel alignment is the setting of the angles of the wheels so that the vehicle drives straight, steers well and wears its tyres evenly. The same words are used in every language of the trade, so it is worth learning them well."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"alignment-angles"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The main angles"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Toe:** the in or out angle of the wheels seen from above. **Toe-in** means the fronts of the wheels are closer than the rears. Wrong toe scrubs the tyre across the road and wears it quickly, usually in a feathered pattern.","**Camber:** the lean of the wheel seen from the front. **Negative camber** means the top leans inwards. Too much wears one edge of the tyre.","**Caster:** the forward or backward tilt of the steering axis seen from the side. Positive caster makes the steering want to return to the straight position and improves stability. Unequal caster makes the vehicle pull to the side with less caster.","**Kingpin (steering axis) inclination:** the inward tilt of the steering axis seen from the front. It helps steering return and is mainly a diagnostic angle that shows a bent part.","**Thrust angle:** the direction in which the rear axle points, compared with the centreline. A wrong thrust angle makes the vehicle 'dog-track' with the rear wheels not following the fronts."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Measuring and adjusting"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Workshops use a wheel alignment machine with sensors or cameras on each wheel. Before the measurement, check the tyre pressures, the wheel bearings, the ball joints and the bushes. A worn part makes any alignment unreliable. Toe is normally adjusted at the tie-rod ends. Camber and caster are adjusted on some cars by eccentric bolts, shims or slotted holes, and on others they are not adjustable and a bent part must be replaced."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Typical alignment figures"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Angle","Typical specification (front)","Typical specification (rear)"],"rows":[["Camber","−0.5° ± 0.5°","−1.0° ± 0.5°"],["Caster","+3° to +5°","Not adjustable"],["Total toe","0° to +0.2° (about 0 to 2 mm toe-in)","+0.1° to +0.3°"],["Thrust angle","Not applicable","0° ± 0.1°"],["Steering axis inclination","About 12° to 14° (a check angle)","Not applicable"]],"caption":"Always use the figures from the manual for the vehicle and the way it is loaded."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What each angle does to the tyres and handling"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Angle","If wrong","Tyre wear","Handling"],"rows":[["Toe-in too much","Front tyres are dragged inward","Feathered wear, worn outer edges","Stable, but sluggish and heavy"],["Toe-out too much","Front tyres are dragged outward","Feathered wear the other way","Darty, unstable on a straight road"],["Too much negative camber","Top of the wheel leans in","Inner edge wear","Good cornering grip but less braking grip"],["Too much positive camber","Top of the wheel leans out","Outer edge wear","Poor cornering grip"],["Unequal caster","The wheel with less caster","Little effect","Pulls toward the side with less caster"],["Too little caster","Steering with little self-centring","Little effect","Wanders, light steering"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Steering axis inclination** (SAI) is the inward lean of the steering axis seen from the front. With camber, it gives the **included angle**, a quick check for bent parts. If the included angle is out of limit on one side while the camber is adjustable, a bent spindle or strut is likely. **Scrub radius** is the distance on the road between the steering axis and the centre of the tyre contact. A large scrub radius makes the steering pull when one tyre brakes harder, or when it hits a bump."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Fix all worn parts before alignment. An alignment done over a worn ball joint or bush is wasted money, because the settings change as soon as the car moves."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A simple check of toe uses a tape measure at wheel height, measured at the front and then the rear of the tyres. It is not exact, but it shows a big error."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l8", "title": "Wheel alignment in practice", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An alignment is a precise measurement and a careful adjustment, and an alignment machine is only as good as the preparation and the technician. This lesson explains the steps of a four-wheel alignment, how to read a printout, how toe is measured in millimetres and degrees, and how to deal with common problems."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Before you align"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check that the vehicle is on a **level lift** or alignment rack, with the turn plates and slip plates free.","Check the **tyres**: the same size and brand on each axle, with the pressures set to the placard and no serious uneven wear.","Check all **steering and suspension parts** for play: ball joints, tie-rod ends, bushes, wheel bearings, rack and dampers. Replace worn parts first, or the alignment will not hold.","Check the **ride height** and that the vehicle has no unusual load. Set the fuel and weight conditions as the manual requires.","Look for bent parts after any collision or a kerb strike."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The steps of a four-wheel alignment"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Fit the sensors or targets to each wheel, and perform the **run-out compensation** of the wheel adapters, as the machine prompts.","Enter the vehicle and its specification. Measure the **rear axle** first, since the thrust angle is the reference for the front.","Adjust the rear camber and toe where the car allows.","Measure the front **caster, camber and toe** on turning the wheels the set angle each way (the caster swing).","Centre the steering wheel and lock it with a holder, then set the front **camber**, then **caster**, and last the **toe**, since toe is the most affected by the other settings.","Tighten the adjusters to torque, and check the readings again, as tightening can change them.","Print the result, showing the **before and after** values, and keep it with the job card."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading a printout"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Measurement","Specification","Before","After","Comment"],"rows":[["Front left camber","−0.5° ± 0.5°","−1.4°","−0.6°","Corrected"],["Front right camber","−0.5° ± 0.5°","−0.4°","−0.4°","Within limit"],["Front total toe","+0.1° ± 0.1°","+0.45°","+0.10°","Corrected"],["Front left caster","+4.0° ± 0.5°","+3.3°","+3.3°","Not adjustable. Check for a bent part."],["Rear total toe","+0.2° ± 0.1°","+0.20°","+0.20°","Within limit"],["Thrust angle","0° ± 0.1°","+0.05°","+0.05°","Within limit"]],"caption":"A good printout shows the readings in green when they are in range. Note the readings that are close to a limit."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Toe in millimetres and degrees"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Toe is a small difference in the distance between the fronts and the rears of two tyres. It can be written as an angle, in degrees, or a distance in millimetres. They are related through the diameter of the tyre or the rim."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Toe (mm) = tyre diameter (mm) × tan (toe angle)"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Converting toe","steps":["A tyre has an outside diameter of 632 mm. A total toe of 0.20° is converted as 632 × tan 0.20° = 632 × 0.00349 = 2.2 mm.","That is about 2 mm of toe-in across the whole axle. A fault of 0.5° is 5.5 mm, enough to scrub off a tyre in a few thousand kilometres.","A tyre that scrubs 5 mm sideways for each wheel turn travels sideways by 5 mm × (1,000 ÷ 1.985) = about 2.5 m for every kilometre driven, which is why bad toe wears a tyre so fast."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Thrust angle and the steering centre"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **thrust angle** is the direction in which the rear axle pushes the car. If it is not straight ahead, the car crabs (**dog-tracks**), with the rear following a different line from the front, and the driver holds the steering wheel off-centre to drive straight. A good alignment sets the rear toe so that the thrust line is straight, and then sets the front relative to it, so that the steering wheel sits centred."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common problems"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Problem","Cause","Remedy"],"rows":[["A reading cannot be adjusted into range","A bent arm, a damaged subframe, or a collapsed bush","Inspect and replace damaged parts, check for a collision history"],["Adjusters seized","Rust on the eccentric bolts or the tie-rod threads","Free with heat or penetrating oil, replace the parts if they are damaged"],["The alignment drifts after a week","Worn joints, loose bolts, an incorrectly tightened bush","Tighten the parts at ride height, and check for wear"],["Steering wheel off-centre after alignment","The wheel was not centred and locked while toe was set","Re-do the toe with the wheel centred"],["The car pulls even after a good alignment","Tyre conicity, different tyre pressures, road crown, a dragging brake","Swap the tyres side to side, check the pressures and brakes"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"After the alignment"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Road test the vehicle. It should go straight on a flat road, with the steering wheel centred and no pull.","Calibrate the **steering angle sensor** if the maker requires it, and check for warning lights.","If the vehicle has driver-assistance systems, check whether a **calibration** is needed after the alignment (module 12).","Give the customer the printout and explain it briefly."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Alignment finds worn parts as much as it sets angles. Always inspect before you align, and print the result."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Never adjust toe before camber and caster. Camber and caster changes affect the toe setting, so set toe last."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l9", "title": "Wheels and balance", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Wheels and tyres are the only parts touching the road. A wheel that is not round, or is out of balance, causes vibration and wears parts out."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wheel balancing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"No wheel and tyre is perfectly even in weight. The heavy spot makes the wheel vibrate as it spins. A balancing machine finds the heavy spots and tells you where to fix small weights on the rim. **Static balance** corrects up-and-down hopping. **Dynamic balance** corrects the side-to-side wobble. Most machines do both together."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Clean the wheel and remove old weights, mud and stones from the tread.","Check the pressure and mount the wheel correctly on the machine, with the right cone.","Enter the rim size and spin the wheel.","Fix the weights where the machine shows, then spin again to confirm."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Runout and damage"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Radial runout** is how far the wheel is out of round. **Lateral runout** is the wobble from side to side. A buckled rim or a bent hub causes vibration that balance weights cannot cure. A damaged rim that is cracked must be replaced and not welded."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure and fitting"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Set the pressure to the value on the vehicle's placard when the tyre is cold. Too low overheats and wears the edges. Too high wears the centre and gives a hard ride.","Wheel **offset** is the distance from the centre line of the rim to the mounting face. A wrong offset changes the track width and can rub on the arch or loads the bearings.","Tighten wheel nuts in a **star pattern** to the specified torque with a torque wrench, and check them again after a short drive."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"How big a weight is needed?","steps":["A wheel has a heavy spot that is equal to an imbalance of 40 g at the rim. A weight of 40 g on the opposite side of the rim at the same radius balances it.","An imbalance of 40 g at a 0.35 m radius spinning at 14 revolutions per second (100 km/h) has a centrifugal force of m × ω² × r. ω = 2π × 14 = 88 rad/s. F = 0.040 × 88² × 0.35 = 0.040 × 7,744 × 0.35 = 108 N.","That force, about 11 kg, rotates once per turn, and hammers the suspension and the steering. At 50 km/h, with half the speed, the force is a quarter, 27 N, so it is not as noticeable. This is why a vibration is felt only in a band of speeds."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fitting wheels and tightening nuts"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Wheel nut type","Seat shape","Used with"],"rows":[["Conical (tapered)","A 60° cone","Most alloy and steel wheels. The wheel centres on the cone."],["Spherical (ball)","A rounded seat","Some European makes"],["Flat with washer","A flat face","Some steel wheels, and some trucks"]],"caption":"The nut must match the wheel's seat. A wrong nut grips on a small area and can come loose."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Clean the hub face and wheel mounting face of rust and dirt, since a rusty face can loosen as the nuts settle.","Check the studs for damage and stretch, and the threads for wear. Replace a stud that is bent or has stripped threads.","Do not oil or grease the threads unless the manual says so, since lubricated threads tighten far more at the same torque.","Run the nuts on by hand, tighten with a wrench in a star pattern, then use a torque wrench for the final torque. Typical values are about 80 to 100 N·m for M12 studs and 110 to 140 N·m for M14 studs, but follow the manual.","Check the torque again after 50 to 100 km, and tell the customer to do so, especially after alloy wheels are fitted or the vehicle has been used on rough roads.","Remember a **spare wheel** that is different from the others. A temporary spare has speed and distance limits and should be removed soon."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A tyre can explode while being inflated. Use a cage or a safe place, never exceed the maximum pressure on the sidewall, and never use a tyre that is cut or bulging."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a vibration appears only at a certain speed, such as 80 to 100 km/h, balance is the first suspect. If it is also in the steering wheel, suspect the front wheels."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l10", "title": "Steering and suspension faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Faults here are usually found by listening to the customer, testing the vehicle and reading the tyres. A system gets you to the answer faster and with fewer wasted parts."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common symptoms"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Possible causes","First checks"],"rows":[["Pull to one side","Uneven tyre pressure, tyre defect, wrong alignment, a dragging brake, a weak spring","Pressures, brake temperature, swap tyres side to side"],["Wander, needs constant correction","Worn tie-rod ends or ball joints, wrong toe or caster, worn bushes","Play checks, alignment"],["Shimmy or shake in the wheel","Out-of-balance wheel, bent rim, worn joints, tyre damage","Balance, runout, play"],["Heavy steering","Low tyre pressure, low fluid, a tight joint, a slipping belt, an electric steering fault","Pressures, fluid, belt, scan tool"],["Knocks, clunks and creaks","Drop links, bushes, ball joints, top mounts","Visual and lever check"],["Fluid leak","Rack seals, hoses, pump","Clean and watch for the source"]],"caption":"Common steering and suspension symptoms"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading tyre wear"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Both edges worn:** under-inflation.","**Centre worn:** over-inflation.","**One edge worn:** too much camber, or worn suspension parts.","**Feathering (ramps across the tread):** wrong toe.","**Cupping or scalloping:** worn dampers or bushes, or an unbalanced wheel."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"A systematic approach"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Ask when the fault happens: at speed, when braking, in corners, hot or cold. Check the pressures and the tyres first because they are the cheapest and most common cause. Then inspect the parts, road-test, and only then measure the alignment. After you change a steering or suspension part, the alignment must be checked, because even new parts change the angles."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Road test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Test the vehicle on a straight, flat road at a safe speed. Release the wheel for a moment, only where it is safe, and see if the car drifts. Brake gently and note any pull or shake. Turn at low speed in both directions and listen for clicks from the drive joints, knocks from the joints and groans from the pump. Drive over a rough patch to reproduce the knocks. Write down what you find, with the speed and the conditions, so that your notes can guide the inspection and the repair."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Customer complaints and where to look"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Complaint","First things to check"],"rows":[["Vehicle pulls to one side","Tyre pressures and wear, alignment (camber and caster differences), dragging brake, worn arm bush"],["Steering wheel off-centre","Alignment toe settings, bent track rod, a worn steering part"],["Heavy steering","Tyre pressure, power steering fluid or belt, electric steering fault, seized joint"],["Steering wheel returns slowly","Tight joint, low caster, a worn column, wrong tyre pressures"],["Clunk over bumps","Drop links, top mounts, ball joints, bushes"],["Wanders at speed","Worn tie-rod ends, wrong toe, tyre wear, play in the rack"],["Wear on the inside or outside edge of a tyre","Camber, toe, worn bushes or damaged parts"],["Body leans in corners","Weak springs, worn dampers, broken anti-roll bar link"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Tyres tell a story. Read the wear pattern before you buy a part, and fix the cause, not only the tyre."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never return a vehicle to the customer with loose, worn or cracked steering parts. If the vehicle is unsafe, explain it clearly in writing."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m18-l11", "title": "Suspension and steering repair procedures", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Suspension and steering work involves heavy parts, compressed springs and high forces, and the quality of the repair directly affects the safety of the driver. This lesson gives the procedures for common jobs and the points that are easy to get wrong. Always follow the workshop manual for the specific vehicle."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing a strut or damper"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Raise and support the vehicle safely on stands, with the wheel off. Support the lower arm or the hub with a jack if the manual says so, so that the brake hose is not stretched.","Disconnect the drop link, the brake hose clips and the ABS sensor wiring, if they are fixed to the strut. Mark any cam bolts that set camber, to put them back in the same position.","Remove the nuts and bolts that fix the strut to the hub. Remove the top mount nuts in the engine bay or boot, and take the strut out.","On a strut with a coil spring, fit a **spring compressor**, in good condition and at the correct points. Compress until the spring is loose, before you remove the top nut.","Take the strut apart. Check the bearing, bump stop and dust boot, and replace worn parts. Fit the new damper, and assemble in the right order, with the spring ends correctly seated.","Release the compressor slowly, and tighten the top nut to torque, holding the rod from turning.","Refit the strut, tighten the nuts and bolts to torque, with the suspension at **ride height** for the bushes.","Fit the wheel, lower the vehicle, and check the alignment."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing a ball joint or a tie-rod end"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Release the nut, and free the taper using a **ball joint separator** or a **fork**, not by hammering the stud's threads.","For a tie-rod end, note the **number of turns** and the length of the thread as you unscrew it, so that you can set the toe close to the old setting.","Some ball joints are **pressed** into the arm. Use the correct tool to press the old one out and the new one in, and do not heat or hammer.","Clean the taper of the stud and the hole. Fit new boots, and use new self-locking or split-pin nuts, tightened to the specified torque.","Fit the nut and the split pin correctly, bending the pin around the nut.","After any steering joint is replaced, check the alignment."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing bushes"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Use a **press**, with the right adaptors, or a bush removal tool. Do not use fire on rubber bushes.","Note the position and direction of any slits or flats in the bush. Align the new bush the same way.","Lubricate the new bush with the lubricant recommended (often rubber lubricant or soap). Avoid oil or grease on rubber.","Tighten the fixing bolts with the wheels on the ground, or the arm raised to ride height, as the manual says."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fastener","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Self-locking nuts and bolts","The locking feature wears. Replace if the manual says so."],["Torque-plus-angle bolts","Stretch bolts that cannot be reused"],["Split pins","Always use a new one"],["Cam bolts and eccentrics","Mark them to keep the camber close"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"A rack replacement outline"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Replacing a steering rack is a major job. It involves disconnecting the column joint, the tie-rod ends, the hoses (hydraulic) or the motor connector (electric), lowering a subframe in some vehicles and re-fitting everything. After the job on a hydraulic rack, bleed the system. On an electric rack, calibrate the angle sensor and torque sensor. Always check the alignment, and do a careful road test. Take care that the steering wheel and the rack are **centred** during the fitting, since a wrongly centred rack will cause the steering wheel to be off-centre or the lock to be uneven."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Safety around the airbag"},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"The steering wheel contains an airbag and a clock spring. Disconnect the battery and wait the time stated before removing the wheel. Never turn the steering with the wheel off, which can break the clock spring."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnostic examples"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 1: Clunk over bumps at the front.** A car has a clunk on speed bumps. Looking at the strut top mount with the car on the ground and the bonnet open, the technician sees the mount moving as an assistant bounces the car. The top mount bearing has collapsed. Replacing the mounts and the dust covers cures it."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 2: Pulls to the left after a kerb strike.** A car pulls to the left after a kerb strike. The alignment printout shows a caster of +1.5° on the left and +4.0° on the right, which is not adjustable. The left lower arm is visibly bent. It is replaced and the alignment re-checked."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 3: Heavy steering and a whine.** A car with hydraulic steering has heavy steering and a whine. The reservoir is nearly empty, and the fluid is foamy. A leak is found at the pressure hose crimp. Replacing the hose, refilling and bleeding the system restores normal steering."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Torque bushes at ride height, replace what the manual says must be replaced, and check the alignment after any steering or suspension repair."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Steering and suspension inspection with a jack and stand","objective":"Inspect the steering and suspension, check play at every joint, and record tyre wear and toe by a simple measurement.","time":"90 minutes","tools":["Vehicle on a lift or on level ground with a jack, axle stands and wheel chocks","Wheel brace, torque wrench, tyre pressure gauge and tyre tread gauge","Pry bar, torch and tape measure","Workshop manual, notepad and a pen"],"safety":["Chock the wheels and use the jacking points in the manual.","Never work under a vehicle supported only by a jack. Use axle stands or a lift and shake the vehicle to check it is stable.","Wear gloves and eye protection. Watch for trapped fingers with a pry bar.","Do not use a spring compressor in this exercise.","Tighten wheel nuts to the correct torque and in a star pattern."],"steps":["Look at the stance of the vehicle on level ground and measure the height of each corner.","Check the tyre pressures and set them to the value on the placard.","Do a bounce test at each corner and note the result.","Record the tyre tread depth in three places across each front tyre, and note the wear pattern.","Raise the front of the vehicle and support it on stands.","Check the wheel bearing play by holding the wheel at the top and bottom and rocking it.","Check the ball joints and the tie-rod ends for play, as the manual says, with the wheel held at 3 and 9 o'clock.","Inspect the dampers, springs, bushes, drop links and gaiters.","Check the steering rack for leaks, and the boots for tears.","Lower the vehicle and measure toe with a tape between the front and rear of the tyres at the wheel centre height.","Write down what is worn, what is safe and what you recommend.","Refit the wheels, torque the nuts and recheck the pressures."],"checks":["I checked the play at each joint using the correct method.","I recorded the wear pattern on the tyres and said what it suggests.","I supported the vehicle safely at all times.","I measured the toe and recorded the figures.","I wrote a clear recommendation for the customer."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m18-q01","q":"What is the job of a damper (shock absorber)?","options":["To carry the weight of the vehicle on the axle by itself","To join the left and right wheels with a single solid bar","To make the suspension stiffer in every kind of corner","To control the movement of the spring so the body does not keep bouncing"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l1","explain":"The spring carries the load and the damper turns its movement into heat by forcing oil through small holes."},
    {"id":"m18-q02","q":"Which suspension type combines the damper and spring in one unit?","options":["The double wishbone, with two arms on each wheel","The torsion beam with a cross member that twists","The MacPherson strut","The solid axle with leaf springs at both ends"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l1","explain":"The MacPherson strut has the spring around the damper, and a lower arm locates the bottom of the wheel."},
    {"id":"m18-q03","q":"What does an anti-roll bar do?","options":["It carries the weight of the vehicle on bumpy roads","It adjusts the toe angle of the front wheels","It controls how fast the springs return after a bump","It reduces body lean in corners by linking the left and right sides"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l1","explain":"It twists when one side moves more than the other, which resists the roll of the body."},
    {"id":"m18-q04","q":"Which parts let the wheel steer and move up and down together?","options":["Wheel nuts and wheel studs on the hub","Drop links on the anti-roll bar","Tie-rod ends fitted to the rack and pinion","Ball joints"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l1","explain":"A ball joint is a swivel that allows two movements at the same time."},
    {"id":"m18-q05","q":"Why do heavy loads wear suspension parts quickly?","options":["They reduce the weight on the bushes and the springs","They make the dampers cooler, so they work less well","They make the tyres larger, so the car sits higher","They compress the springs and change the alignment, which loads the joints and bushes more"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l1","explain":"Overloading is common on our roads and is a regular cause of early failure."},
    {"id":"m18-q06","q":"A car's rear left corner sits lower than the right. What should you suspect first?","options":["Too much air in the tyre on the high side","A weak or broken spring on the low side","A wrong toe setting on the rear axle of the car","A worn tie-rod end on the front left wheel"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l1","explain":"Stance is a quick clue. A tired or broken spring is the first thing to check."},
    {"id":"m18-q07","q":"What is the unsprung mass?","options":["The body, seats and engine","Wheels, tyres and brakes","The fuel and the passengers","The springs only"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l1","explain":"Unsprung mass is what is not carried by the springs. Lighter unsprung mass lets the wheel follow the road better."},
    {"id":"m18-q08","q":"A corner carries 350 kg on a wheel rate of 25 N/mm. What is the ride frequency?","options":["About 13 Hz","About 0.13 Hz","About 130 Hz","About 1.3 Hz"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l2","explain":"f = 1 ÷ 2π × √(25,000 ÷ 350) = 0.159 × 8.45 = 1.34 Hz, near the comfortable range."},
    {"id":"m18-q09","q":"What happens to ride frequency when a vehicle is loaded?","options":["It falls and the ride gets softer","It rises and the ride gets harsher","It stays exactly the same","It becomes zero"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l2","explain":"More mass on the same spring lowers the natural frequency. The body also sits lower."},
    {"id":"m18-q10","q":"A 1,400 kg car corners at 0.6 g, with a CG height of 0.55 m and a track of 1.5 m. About how much weight transfers?","options":["About 300 N, or about 31 kg","About 30,000 N, or 3,080 kg","About 3,000 N, or 308 kg","About 30 N, or about 3 kg"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l2","explain":"Transfer = 1,400 × 5.89 × 0.55 ÷ 1.5 = 3,021 N, about 308 kg moved from the inside to the outside tyres."},
    {"id":"m18-q11","q":"What is understeer?","options":["The front loses grip first","The rear tyres lose grip first","The car turns tighter than the driver asks","The steering feels light at speed"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l2","explain":"In understeer the car does not turn as much as the driver asks, since the front tyres have less grip."},
    {"id":"m18-q12","q":"How do you do a bounce test?","options":["Push down hard on a corner and release it, and see how many times the body rises","Raise the vehicle and spin the wheel by hand","Measure the height of the car with the doors open","Drive over a kerb at speed and listen for a knock"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l3","explain":"A good damper allows one rise and a settle. More bounces show a worn damper."},
    {"id":"m18-q13","q":"When should the arm bushes be fully tightened?","options":["With the wheels hanging after the vehicle is raised on a lift","With the engine running and the wheels turned to full lock","With the vehicle on its wheels at ride height","Before the new bushes are pressed into the arms"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l3","explain":"Tightening with the wheels hanging twists the rubber, which tears it early when the load is on."},
    {"id":"m18-q14","q":"Why should dampers and springs be replaced in pairs on the same axle?","options":["So the left and right sides behave the same","Because the manufacturer sells them only in pairs of two","Because the old ones are always damaged by the new ones","Because each one cannot be fitted without the second"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l3","explain":"Different damping on each side gives uneven handling and braking."},
    {"id":"m18-q15","q":"A car knocks over bumps from the front. Which parts are common causes?","options":["The air filter, the spark plugs and the ignition coils","The wiper blades, the washer jets and the windscreen","Worn drop links, ball joints, top mounts or dampers","The brake discs, pads and the master cylinder reservoir"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l3","explain":"Knocks over bumps come from worn joints and mounts that move more than they should."},
    {"id":"m18-q16","q":"What is the biggest danger of removing a coil spring from a strut?","options":["The spring will be hot and can cause a burn","The strut will fall off and damage the tyre","The stored energy of the compressed spring can cause serious injury","The spring will be too heavy to lift by hand"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l3","explain":"Use a good spring compressor, fitted correctly, and release the nut only when the spring is fully held."},
    {"id":"m18-q17","q":"What does a damper resist more: bump or rebound?","options":["Bump","Neither","Rebound","Both equally"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l4","explain":"A damper is set to resist the extension of the spring more than its compression, so the body settles without bouncing and the wheel can move up easily over bumps."},
    {"id":"m18-q18","q":"A damper is leaking a wet streak of oil down its body. What does this mean?","options":["It is normal and needs no action","It needs only a clean","It has too much oil","It has failed: replace it"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l4","explain":"A damp film is normal, but a wet leak with dust shows the seal has failed. It will have lost damping."},
    {"id":"m18-q19","q":"Before supporting a car with air suspension on stands, what should be done?","options":["Fill the bags to maximum","Put it in jacking mode","Remove the compressor fuse only","Nothing is needed"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l4","explain":"The system may lower the car as it detects a lack of load, which can trap you. Switch it to the mode the manual specifies."},
    {"id":"m18-q20","q":"An air-suspension car sags at one corner overnight. What is the likely cause?","options":["A weak battery in the engine bay","A worn spark plug in one cylinder","A flat tyre on the same corner","A leak in a bag or line"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l4","explain":"The air escapes slowly. Find it with soapy water or the scan tool's leak test."},
    {"id":"m18-q21","q":"What does a rack-and-pinion steering gear do?","options":["It holds the wheel at the correct camber angle","It turns the rotation of the engine into drive for the wheels","It turns the rotation of the steering wheel into a sideways movement of the tie rods","It controls the damping of the front springs on bumps"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l5","explain":"A small pinion gear meshes with a toothed bar, the rack, and moves it left and right."},
    {"id":"m18-q22","q":"Which steering system needs no power steering fluid?","options":["A steering box with a worm gear in a closed case of oil","Hydraulic power steering with an engine-driven pump","Electric power steering (EPS)","Electro-hydraulic steering with a fluid reservoir"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l5","explain":"EPS uses an electric motor and a torque sensor, controlled by a computer, so there is no fluid."},
    {"id":"m18-q23","q":"Before removing the steering wheel on a car with an airbag, what must you do?","options":["Probe the airbag wiring to check that it is dead","Disconnect the battery and wait for the time in the manual","Turn the wheel to full lock and pull it straight off","Leave the battery connected so the memory is not lost"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l5","explain":"The airbag circuit can hold charge, so you wait. Never probe SRS wiring."},
    {"id":"m18-q24","q":"A tie rod is bent. What should you do?","options":["Replace it, never heat or weld it","Weld a reinforcing plate over the bent area","Refit it and adjust the toe to hide the error","Straighten it with a hammer and heat it to remove the bend"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l5","explain":"Steering parts are safety parts. Heating or welding changes the strength of the metal in a hidden way."},
    {"id":"m18-q25","q":"Why does the inner wheel turn through a larger angle in a corner?","options":["It carries less weight than the outer","It follows a smaller circle","It has a smaller tyre fitted to it","It is nearer the engine and gearbox"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l6","explain":"The Ackermann arrangement steers the inner wheel more, so that both wheels roll without scrubbing."},
    {"id":"m18-q26","q":"A steering wheel turns 1,080° lock to lock and the road wheels turn 70°. What is the ratio?","options":["About 0.065 to 1","About 70 to 1","About 15.4 to 1","About 1 to 1"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l6","explain":"Ratio = 1,080 ÷ 70 = 15.4. A lower ratio gives a quicker steering response."},
    {"id":"m18-q27","q":"Hydraulic steering whines when turning and the reservoir is low. What should you check?","options":["Leaks, and the pump and belt","The brake pads on all the wheels","The air conditioning compressor","The tyre tread depth on the front"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l6","explain":"Low fluid makes the pump suck in air and whine. Find the leak, top up and bleed."},
    {"id":"m18-q28","q":"What should be done after replacing an electric steering rack?","options":["Refill with power steering fluid again","Calibrate the angle and torque sensors","Replace the engine oil and filter","Check the exhaust system for leaks"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l6","explain":"Electric systems have no fluid. The sensors must be zeroed with a scan tool so the module knows the centre."},
    {"id":"m18-q29","q":"Why does a low battery voltage matter for electric steering?","options":["It can reduce the assistance","It makes the steering lighter","It improves the feel","It has no effect"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l6","explain":"The motor needs a good supply. A weak battery may give a warning and no assistance."},
    {"id":"m18-q30","q":"What does the term toe mean in wheel alignment?","options":["The lean of the top of the wheel when seen from the front","The angle between the rear axle and the centre line","The forward or backward tilt of the steering axis","The angle of the wheels in or out when seen from above"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l7","explain":"Toe-in means the fronts of the wheels are closer together than the rears."},
    {"id":"m18-q31","q":"Wrong toe usually causes which tyre wear?","options":["Wear on the centre of the tread only","Feathering across the tread","Wear on the inside shoulder only","Wear on both edges at the same time"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l7","explain":"The tyre is dragged sideways, which makes small ramps in the tread."},
    {"id":"m18-q32","q":"What does too much negative camber wear out?","options":["The tread across the whole width evenly","The inside edge of the tyre","The outside edge of the tyre","The centre of the tread across the tyre"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l7","explain":"Negative camber leans the top of the wheel inwards, so the inner edge carries more load."},
    {"id":"m18-q33","q":"What does positive caster do for the steering?","options":["It brings the front of the wheels closer together","It makes the car lean less in corners","It helps the steering return to straight and improves stability","It makes the tyres lean in at the top for cornering"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l7","explain":"A vehicle pulls to the side that has less caster."},
    {"id":"m18-q34","q":"Why should worn parts be fixed before an alignment?","options":["Settings made over worn parts change as soon as the car moves","Because the machine will refuse to measure with any wear","Because the new tyres have to be run in first","Because worn parts are always adjusted by the machine"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l7","explain":"Alignment must be done on sound parts to be reliable."},
    {"id":"m18-q35","q":"Where is toe usually adjusted?","options":["At the tie-rod ends","At the wheel nuts, by loosening them","At the top of the coil spring","At the anti-roll bar drop links"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l7","explain":"The outer tie rod threads in or out to change the length and so the toe."},
    {"id":"m18-q36","q":"What should be done first before an alignment?","options":["Adjust the toe to the middle","Adjust the camber on the right side","Check tyres and worn parts","Paint the wheels a different colour"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l8","explain":"Worn parts make any alignment unstable. Check tyres, pressures, play and ride height before you start."},
    {"id":"m18-q37","q":"A tyre is 632 mm in diameter. What does a toe of 0.20° equal?","options":["About 22 mm","About 0.22 mm","About 220 mm","About 2.2 mm"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l8","explain":"Toe = 632 × tan 0.20° = 632 × 0.00349 = 2.2 mm."},
    {"id":"m18-q38","q":"Which angle is set last on a front alignment?","options":["Toe","Camber","Caster","Thrust"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l8","explain":"Camber and caster changes alter toe. Toe is set last, with the steering wheel centred and locked."},
    {"id":"m18-q39","q":"The caster is not adjustable and is off by 2.5° on one side. What is the probable cause?","options":["The wrong tyre pressure","A flat battery","A blocked filter","A bent or damaged arm"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l8","explain":"If a fixed angle is out of range, a bent or collapsed component is the usual reason, often after a kerb strike."},
    {"id":"m18-q40","q":"What does thrust angle describe?","options":["The angle of the steering wheel","The tyre tread depth","Where the rear axle points","The wheel offset"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l8","explain":"If it is not straight ahead, the car dog-tracks and the steering wheel sits off-centre."},
    {"id":"m18-q41","q":"A customer says the steering wheel shakes at 90 km/h but is smooth at 50. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A low level of windscreen washer fluid in the bottle","An out-of-balance front wheel","A weak battery that cannot hold the voltage","A fault in the air conditioning compressor clutch"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l9","explain":"Vibrations that appear at a certain speed are a classic sign of imbalance."},
    {"id":"m18-q42","q":"What does dynamic balance correct?","options":["The side-to-side wobble of the wheel","The pressure difference between two tyres","The offset of the rim on the hub","Only the up-and-down hopping of the wheel"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l9","explain":"Static balance corrects hopping and dynamic balance corrects wobble. Most machines do both."},
    {"id":"m18-q43","q":"When should the tyre pressure be set to the placard value?","options":["After the wheel nuts have been loosened","After a long fast drive when the tyre is hot","Only when the vehicle is fully loaded with cargo","When the tyre is cold"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l9","explain":"Pressure rises as the tyre warms, so a hot reading gives a false value."},
    {"id":"m18-q44","q":"In which order should wheel nuts be tightened?","options":["In a circle, starting with the nut at the top","In a star pattern, as tightly as possible with an impact gun","In a star pattern, to the specified torque","Randomly, so the wheel centres itself on the studs"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l9","explain":"A star pattern pulls the wheel evenly onto the hub, and a torque wrench prevents damage."},
    {"id":"m18-q45","q":"Why should you never use a tyre that is bulging?","options":["It will make the speedometer read too high","It will wear faster, but it is otherwise safe to use","It will make more noise on a smooth road surface","The cords inside are damaged, so it can burst without warning"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l9","explain":"A bulge shows internal damage. Replace the tyre."},
    {"id":"m18-q46","q":"A 40 g imbalance at a 0.35 m radius spins at 14 revolutions per second. What is the approximate force?","options":["About 108 N","About 1 N","About 1,080 N","About 10 N"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l9","explain":"F = m ω² r = 0.040 × 88² × 0.35 = 108 N. The force grows with the square of speed."},
    {"id":"m18-q47","q":"Both edges of a tyre are worn. What is the cause?","options":["Too much toe-in on that axle","Under-inflation","A bent steering arm on the knuckle","Over-inflation of the tyre"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l10","explain":"A low-pressure tyre sags, so the edges carry the load and wear faster."},
    {"id":"m18-q48","q":"A vehicle pulls to the left. Which check is the cheapest and should be made first?","options":["Remove and replace the steering rack and pinion before any other checks","Replace both of the front springs on the axle","Replace the front dampers on both sides","The tyre pressures, and swap the tyres from side to side"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l10","explain":"Pressure and tyre faults are common causes. A dragging brake is also possible."},
    {"id":"m18-q49","q":"A car has heavy steering with a hydraulic system and a squeal on turning. What should you check?","options":["The fluid level and the drive belt","The coolant level and the thermostat","The fuel pressure and the injectors","The air filter and the throttle body"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l10","explain":"Low fluid or a slipping belt makes the pump noisy and the steering heavy."},
    {"id":"m18-q50","q":"After changing a ball joint, what must be done before returning the car to the customer?","options":["Wash the vehicle and the wheels","Check the wheel alignment","Only re-check the pressures in the tyres","Nothing, because new parts do not affect alignment"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l10","explain":"Even new parts change the angles, so alignment must be checked."},
    {"id":"m18-q51","q":"A tyre has scalloped, cupped wear. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Too much air in the tyre for a very long time","Worn dampers or bushes, or an out-of-balance wheel","A loose air filter housing on the engine","An engine misfire at idle"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l10","explain":"The wheel bounces on the road at a regular interval, which makes the cups."},
    {"id":"m18-q52","q":"A mechanic finds a cracked steering part on a customer's car. What is the right action?","options":["Tell the customer it is unsafe, recommend replacement and write it down","Return the car and say nothing at all so as not to worry the customer","Weld the crack and return the car the same day","Tell the customer to come back when the crack has grown"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l10","explain":"Safety defects must be recorded and explained clearly, in writing."},
    {"id":"m18-q53","q":"A car pulls to one side after a new tyre is fitted. Where should you look first?","options":["The engine oil level and grade","The windscreen wipers and jets","Tyre pressures and condition","The radio and the speakers"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l10","explain":"A new tyre with conicity, or wrong pressures, can cause a pull. Swap tyres and check pressures before you adjust the alignment."},
    {"id":"m18-q54","q":"When should rubber bushes be fully tightened?","options":["With the wheels hanging","At ride height","With the car on a lift","Before fitting"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l11","explain":"Tightening with the wheels hanging twists the rubber. It tears early in service."},
    {"id":"m18-q55","q":"What should always be fitted with a new split-pin castle nut?","options":["The old split pin","A cable tie","A piece of wire","A new split pin"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m18-l11","explain":"Split pins deform and cannot be reliably reused."},
    {"id":"m18-q56","q":"Why note the turns on a tie-rod end as it is removed?","options":["To count the teeth on the nut","To measure the tyre pressure","To restore the toe roughly","To time the engine"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m18-l11","explain":"Fitting the new end with the same turns gives about the same toe so the car can be driven to the alignment machine."},
    {"id":"m18-q57","q":"What must be done before removing the steering wheel?","options":["Spray the airbag with oil","Disconnect the battery and wait","Turn the wheel to full lock and back","Remove the horn"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m18-l11","explain":"The wheel carries an airbag. Remove the power and wait for it to drain, as the manual says."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m18-c01","front":"What does a damper do?","back":"It controls the movement of the spring so the body does not keep bouncing.","lesson":"m18-l1"},
    {"id":"m18-c02","front":"What does an anti-roll bar do?","back":"It reduces body lean in corners.","lesson":"m18-l1"},
    {"id":"m18-c03","front":"What is a MacPherson strut?","back":"A strut that combines the damper and spring in one unit.","lesson":"m18-l1"},
    {"id":"m18-c04","front":"What are bushes for?","back":"They absorb noise and allow small movements at arm joints.","lesson":"m18-l1"},
    {"id":"m18-c05","front":"What is a ball joint?","back":"A swivel joint that lets the wheel steer and move up and down.","lesson":"m18-l1"},
    {"id":"m18-c06","front":"Natural frequency formula?","back":"f = 1 ÷ 2π × √(spring rate ÷ mass). Comfortable is about 1.0 to 1.5 Hz.","lesson":"m18-l2"},
    {"id":"m18-c07","front":"Weight transfer formula?","back":"Mass × lateral acceleration × CG height ÷ track width.","lesson":"m18-l2"},
    {"id":"m18-c08","front":"What are understeer and oversteer?","back":"Understeer: the front loses grip and the car runs wide. Oversteer: the rear loses grip and the tail slides.","lesson":"m18-l2"},
    {"id":"m18-c09","front":"What does a bounce test show?","back":"The condition of the damper. A good one rises once and settles.","lesson":"m18-l3"},
    {"id":"m18-c10","front":"When are arm bushes fully tightened?","back":"With the vehicle on its wheels at ride height.","lesson":"m18-l3"},
    {"id":"m18-c11","front":"Why replace dampers in pairs?","back":"So the left and right sides act the same.","lesson":"m18-l3"},
    {"id":"m18-c12","front":"What is the danger of a coil spring?","back":"A compressed spring stores huge energy and can injure you.","lesson":"m18-l3"},
    {"id":"m18-c13","front":"What is the job of a damper?","back":"It forces oil through small valves to control the movement of the spring, and resists rebound more than bump.","lesson":"m18-l4"},
    {"id":"m18-c14","front":"Twin-tube and monotube dampers?","back":"Twin-tube is common and comfortable. Monotube has high-pressure gas, cools better and is firmer.","lesson":"m18-l4"},
    {"id":"m18-c15","front":"Safety with air suspension?","back":"Switch to jacking or transport mode before lifting or supporting the vehicle.","lesson":"m18-l4"},
    {"id":"m18-c16","front":"What does a rack and pinion do?","back":"It turns the steering wheel rotation into side movement of the tie rods.","lesson":"m18-l5"},
    {"id":"m18-c17","front":"What are the three power steering types?","back":"Hydraulic, electro-hydraulic and electric.","lesson":"m18-l5"},
    {"id":"m18-c18","front":"What must you do before removing an airbag steering wheel?","back":"Disconnect the battery and wait as the manual says.","lesson":"m18-l5"},
    {"id":"m18-c19","front":"Can you weld a bent steering part?","back":"No. Replace it.","lesson":"m18-l5"},
    {"id":"m18-c20","front":"Steering ratio formula?","back":"Steering wheel travel from lock to lock divided by road wheel travel. 1,080° ÷ 70° is 15.4 to 1.","lesson":"m18-l5"},
    {"id":"m18-c21","front":"What is the Ackermann principle?","back":"The inner wheel turns through a larger angle than the outer, so both roll without scrubbing.","lesson":"m18-l6"},
    {"id":"m18-c22","front":"Typical hydraulic steering pressure?","back":"About 80 to 120 bar at full lock.","lesson":"m18-l6"},
    {"id":"m18-c23","front":"What must be calibrated on electric steering?","back":"The steering angle sensor and the torque sensor, with a scan tool.","lesson":"m18-l6"},
    {"id":"m18-c24","front":"What is toe?","back":"The in or out angle of the wheels seen from above.","lesson":"m18-l7"},
    {"id":"m18-c25","front":"What is camber?","back":"The lean of the wheel seen from the front.","lesson":"m18-l7"},
    {"id":"m18-c26","front":"What is caster?","back":"The forward or backward tilt of the steering axis seen from the side.","lesson":"m18-l7"},
    {"id":"m18-c27","front":"What does wrong toe do to a tyre?","back":"It wears the tyre quickly in a feathered pattern.","lesson":"m18-l7"},
    {"id":"m18-c28","front":"What is thrust angle?","back":"The direction in which the rear axle points, compared with the centreline.","lesson":"m18-l7"},
    {"id":"m18-c29","front":"Order of setting a front alignment?","back":"Camber, then caster, then toe last, with the steering wheel centred.","lesson":"m18-l8"},
    {"id":"m18-c30","front":"Toe formula?","back":"Toe (mm) = tyre diameter × tan (toe angle).","lesson":"m18-l8"},
    {"id":"m18-c31","front":"What does the thrust angle describe?","back":"The direction the rear axle pushes the car. If it is off, the car dog-tracks.","lesson":"m18-l8"},
    {"id":"m18-c32","front":"Why check parts before an alignment?","back":"Worn parts make the alignment unstable and unreliable.","lesson":"m18-l8"},
    {"id":"m18-c33","front":"What is dynamic balance?","back":"Correcting the side-to-side wobble of the wheel.","lesson":"m18-l9"},
    {"id":"m18-c34","front":"What is runout?","back":"How far a wheel is out of round or has a side wobble.","lesson":"m18-l9"},
    {"id":"m18-c35","front":"How are wheel nuts tightened?","back":"In a star pattern to the specified torque.","lesson":"m18-l9"},
    {"id":"m18-c36","front":"What does a wheel offset affect?","back":"The track width and the loading of bearings.","lesson":"m18-l9"},
    {"id":"m18-c37","front":"Why is vibration felt only in a band of speeds?","back":"Force from imbalance grows with the square of speed and excites a resonance at certain speeds.","lesson":"m18-l9"},
    {"id":"m18-c38","front":"What does both edges worn show?","back":"Under-inflation.","lesson":"m18-l10"},
    {"id":"m18-c39","front":"What does centre wear show?","back":"Over-inflation.","lesson":"m18-l10"},
    {"id":"m18-c40","front":"What does feathering show?","back":"Wrong toe.","lesson":"m18-l10"},
    {"id":"m18-c41","front":"What must follow replacing a steering or suspension part?","back":"A wheel alignment check.","lesson":"m18-l10"},
    {"id":"m18-c42","front":"What do you check first for a pull?","back":"The tyre pressures and tyre condition.","lesson":"m18-l10"},
    {"id":"m18-c43","front":"What causes cupped tyre wear?","back":"Worn dampers or bushes, or an unbalanced wheel.","lesson":"m18-l10"},
    {"id":"m18-c44","front":"Torque rubber bushes where?","back":"With the suspension at ride height.","lesson":"m18-l11"},
    {"id":"m18-c45","front":"How to free a ball joint taper?","back":"Use a separator or fork. Do not hammer the stud threads.","lesson":"m18-l11"},
    {"id":"m18-c46","front":"Always renew after removal?","back":"Split pins, self-locking nuts, torque-plus-angle bolts and torn boots.","lesson":"m18-l11"}
  ]
});
